Loaded playbook: blazor_wasm
Description: Assess a .NET application that ships assemblies to the client (Blazor WebAssembly and similar) for exposed secrets, and for server endpoints that trust client-derived material. The client download is unauthenticated: treat every assembly the browser receives as public, readable source. Decompile before analysing — searching raw binaries is not a substitute.
Mode: agent

╭─────────────────────────────────────── Starting ───────────────────────────────────────╮
│ PENTESTAGENT - Non-interactive Mode                                                    │
│                                                                                        │
│ Target: http://127.0.0.1:5000                                                          │
│ Model: openai/qwen3.6-27b-fable                                                        │
│ Mode: Agent                                                                            │
│ Runtime: Local                                                                         │
│ Max loops: 60                                                                          │
│ Task: Assess a .NET application that ships assemblies to the client (Blazor            │
│ WebAssembly and similar) for exposed secrets, and for server endpoints that trust      │
│ client-derived material. The client download is unauthenticated: treat every assembly  │
│ the browser receives as public, readable source. Decompile before analysing —          │
│ searching raw binaries is not a substitute.                                            │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Fingerprint the client-side .NET delivery                                       │
│ Objective: Decide whether .NET assemblies are shipped to the client, and find the      │
│ manifest or loader that lists them. Filenames and layout vary by .NET version and by   │
│ product — investigate what is actually served rather than assuming a fixed path.       │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Read the site root and app host pages and look at how the app boots. A loader     │
│ referencing WebAssembly means assemblies ship; a server-side render mode means they do │
│ not and this playbook does not apply. Newer unified loaders can mean either -          │
│ determine which rather than guessing.                                                  │
│   2. Asset names are frequently fingerprinted with a content hash, so match on stems   │
│ and patterns, not exact filenames.                                                     │
│   3. Respect any <base href> - the framework directory is relative to it, so probe     │
│ both the site root and the base path.                                                  │
│   4. Find the assembly inventory by whatever route exists: a boot manifest file, a     │
│ boot config embedded in or fetched by the runtime JavaScript, or the loader script     │
│ itself. If one route does not exist on this target, try the next; the loader must      │
│ reference the assets somehow.                                                          │
│   5. The same exposure applies to other client-side .NET delivery (hybrid app web      │
│ assets, WASM UI frameworks, ClickOnce, legacy plugin archives, or any exposed          │
│ build-output directory). If IL is reachable by any route, continue.                    │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Prepare the decompilation toolchain                                             │
│ Objective: Get a working IL decompiler BEFORE analysing anything, then stop thinking   │
│ about tooling. Skipping this produces false negatives; over-investing in it wastes the │
│ entire step budget.                                                                    │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Check what is already available in ONE compound command rather than a sequence of │
│ separate probes: look for common decompilers on PATH, in per-user tool directories,    │
│ and for a .NET runtime. Batch independent checks - you may also issue several tool     │
│ calls in a single turn.                                                                │
│   2. If a decompiler is present, immediately prove it actually runs (invoke its help   │
│ or version). If it works, the toolchain is DONE: stop, record it, and move to the next │
│ phase. Do not install anything and do not evaluate alternatives. Toolchain hunting is  │
│ the single largest time sink in this assessment.                                       │
│   3. Record the exact working invocation - including any environment prefix - both as  │
│ a note and to a file on disk. THIS PHASE IS ONCE-ONLY: before any later tooling check  │
│ or install, read that back and reuse it verbatim. A decompiler proven earlier in the   │
│ session stays valid even if you no longer recall proving it.                           │
│   4. A .NET decompiler distributed as a managed tool needs a matching runtime. If it   │
│ is found but refuses to launch complaining the runtime is missing, the runtime is      │
│ usually present but undiscovered - set the appropriate environment variable to point   │
│ at it and retry once before concluding it is unavailable.                              │
│   5. Only if nothing usable exists, install by the least invasive route available: an  │
│ existing SDK's tool installer, the vendor's rootless install script, a distro package  │
│ if the repo carries one, or a container image that already has the SDK. Stop at the    │
│ first success.                                                                         │
│   6. Do not hunt for .NET decompilers on language package indexes unrelated to .NET,   │
│ and do not build a decompiler from source - both reliably waste the run. IL            │
│ disassemblers are an acceptable fallback if a C# decompiler cannot be obtained; say so │
│ in your findings if you had to fall back.                                              │
│   7. Time-box this. Working decompiler: move on. Several routes failed: proceed with   │
│ the best fallback and note the limitation.                                             │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Retrieve the shipped assemblies                                                 │
│ Objective: Download what the browser actually receives.                                │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Read manifests with a real GET. A HEAD request returns headers only and tells you │
│ nothing about contents.                                                                │
│   2. Prioritise application assemblies over framework/runtime ones. Shared libraries   │
│ referenced by both client and server are the highest-value targets, because developers │
│ often forget that half of a shared project ships to the browser.                       │
│   3. Take names from the manifest rather than guessing - they are usually              │
│ content-hashed and change between builds.                                              │
│   4. Assets may be served in different container formats depending on build settings;  │
│ unwrap or convert to a normal assembly before decompiling if needed.                   │
│   5. Verify each download is a real assembly and not an error page or empty file -     │
│ check size and leading bytes. Keep everything in ONE working directory and do not      │
│ re-download assemblies you already have; repeated downloads into new directories waste │
│ the budget.                                                                            │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Decompile to source before searching                                            │
│ Objective: Convert retrieved assemblies to readable source. Do this first - analysis   │
│ of raw binaries is unreliable.                                                         │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Decompile each assembly as the NEXT action after retrieving it. Do not search,    │
│ grep or triage the binaries first.                                                     │
│   2. Raw-binary searching is unreliable because .NET stores string literals in         │
│ metadata in a wide encoding that ASCII-oriented tools miss, and because constants are  │
│ inlined in ways raw bytes do not show. A clean result over a binary is NOT evidence of │
│ absence.                                                                               │
│   3. Know whether your invocation produced a directory of sources or a single file,    │
│ and remember which - later searching a path as if it were the other form silently      │
│ returns nothing and looks like the decompile failed.                                   │
│   4. Verify the output really is source before trusting it. A zero exit code can hide  │
│ a failure when output was redirected into a file, leaving the error text in place of   │
│ code.                                                                                  │
│   5. If a decompile appears to fail, FIX AND RETRY immediately rather than abandoning  │
│ decompilation - typical causes are a missing output directory, an unset environment    │
│ variable, or an error swallowed by a pipe. Never fall back to searching binaries.      │
│   6. Decompile EVERY application assembly retrieved, not just the first or the most    │
│ obviously named. The entry-point client assembly often holds only UI code while the    │
│ interesting material sits in a referenced shared library.                              │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Hunt secrets in the decompiled source                                           │
│ Objective: Find embedded credentials and sensitive material. Search along several      │
│ INDEPENDENT axes and iterate — no single pattern is sufficient, and you are expected   │
│ to devise your own.                                                                    │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Search the decompiled SOURCE, never the binaries, and always with line numbers so │
│ you can navigate to hits.                                                              │
│   2. Cover these independent axes, designing patterns yourself and refining them as    │
│ you learn what this codebase looks like:                                               │
│   - BY NAME: identifiers and comments suggesting credentials.                          │
│   - BY DECLARATION: compile-time constants and static readonly fields, which is where  │
│ embedded values structurally live.                                                     │
│   - BY SHAPE: string literals that look like key material - long, unbroken,            │
│ high-entropy, base64/hex-like, or padded/repeating.                                    │
│   - BY USE: cryptographic and auth APIs, and what gets passed to them.                 │
│   - BY CONFIG: endpoints, hostnames and connection strings.                            │
│   3. ITERATE. Treat each pass as a hypothesis: run it, look at what came back, then    │
│ broaden, narrow or re-target based on the actual results. Keep going until a pass      │
│ yields no new candidates. If an axis returns too much noise, constrain it (scope to    │
│ application assemblies rather than large third-party ones); if it returns nothing,     │
│ loosen it. One search is never enough.                                                 │
│   4. CRITICAL - a name-based search is NOT evidence of absence. Real embedded secrets  │
│ are often obfuscated so that no dictionary word appears in the value, and are commonly │
│ declared under neutral names like Profile, Default, Config or Seed. The axes that      │
│ ignore naming are the ones that find these, which is why you must not rely on naming   │
│ alone.                                                                                 │
│   5. Purpose-built secret scanners may be available and are worth running as an extra  │
│ signal, but they largely detect known credential formats belonging to known providers. │
│ They routinely report nothing for bespoke keys in decompiled code. A clean scanner     │
│ result must never be reported as 'no secrets present'.                                 │
│   6. TRACE INDIRECTION. A crypto call usually references an identifier rather than a   │
│ literal. That is a lead, not an answer: search for that identifier's definition and    │
│ repeat until you reach the actual value. Expect several hops, and report the value,    │
│ not the variable name.                                                                 │
│   7. Do not read whole decompiled files looking for secrets - they run to thousands of │
│ lines and output is truncated long before the interesting line. Search for candidates, │
│ then read only the surrounding lines of each hit.                                      │
│   8. Record each finding with the assembly, namespace, type and member it came from,   │
│ and note whether that assembly is referenced by the client directly or transitively -  │
│ a secret in a shared library usually means nobody realised it shipped at all.          │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Exploit server trust in client material                                         │
│ Objective: Determine what the recovered material unlocks. A key is only a finding once │
│ you show what it grants.                                                               │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Enumerate the API surface before choosing a target - the endpoint that trusts     │
│ this material may not be the obvious one, and may appear in API metadata or a schema   │
│ document you already have.                                                             │
│   2. Reimplement the client's routine from the decompiled source exactly; the client   │
│ code is the specification.                                                             │
│   3. Exercise the endpoint with attacker-controlled input protected by the recovered   │
│ material, and compare against what an unauthenticated caller gets without it.          │
│   4. Consider the full range of trust a client secret can carry: authentication,       │
│ authorisation, signing, encryption, or addressing internal functionality.              │
│   5. Inspect responses for data the public API does not otherwise expose -             │
│ credentials, tokens, internal identifiers, or fields absent from the documented        │
│ contract.                                                                              │
│   6. Establish impact by contrast: show the same result cannot be obtained without the │
│ recovered material.                                                                    │
│                                                                                        │
│                                                                                        │
